WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.400
I grew up here in Portland.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.200
As a child, we all love Indiana Jones.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.240
But it was actually really in high school

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.320
when I was able to take an anthropology
class and it really piqued my interest.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.880
And then in community college,
I started taking archeology classes.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.640
The coast is not forgiving
to the artifacts that we're finding.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.560
They're often broken up
small chunks of what we would find.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.520
Even though we've got only fragments,

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.640
they can really tell us
where they were made, when

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.520
and even who for.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:42.360
The first thing you notice

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.080
about these blue color designs
is that they are Chinese.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.280
So why are Chinese porcelains
being brought to Oregon?

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.080
We found the shards were from large
plates and cups matching

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.320
European desire for large meals
in months of hot chocolate.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.640
This is likely a cup, we

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.800
don't really know what the foot rim was
meant to be or what the purpose of it is.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:08.160
But apparently it was a very big thing
with the European market at the time.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.200
Using all this data together,

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.760
we have come to a date of 1690

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.520
and what's intriguing
is that it's 120 years

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.800
before any Europeans arrived in Oregon.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.080
That beach on that side, there's
a lot of porcelain that seems to be right.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.000
So basically what you're seeing
is material like directly

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.520
on the opposite side of the
where we currently are.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.080
Yeah.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.920
And you've recovered some porcelain.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.480
Yeah. Just north of here.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.000
Lots of it. Yeah.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.280
So, you know,
you go here to there to there.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:43.800
It kind of lines up, you know.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.760
This is an underwater environment known

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.360
by local fishermen
as a place where they've hung nets.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.440
And that's always a great indication of

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.760
of a place
that could be parts of a shipwreck.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.800
Are we clear?

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:58.440
That's perfect.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.480
Okay.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.840
We're looking at the data
as it's being collected

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.200
and nothing was obviously sticking out.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.440
I should have more coffee this morning.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.600
And suddenly we saw this one area where

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.440
there was this linear feature.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:20.800
It looked almost like the spines
on the back of a stegosaurus.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.400
There's definitely something out here.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.360
We're starting to pick up already

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:28.680
some linear features in an otherwise 
sandy environment.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.840
Yeah, that doesn't really look like rock.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:31.840
Well, here already.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.200
Yeah. Here we’re
starting to pick up and see more of this.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.200
Yeah, it is four or five meters long,
something like that.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.720
Maybe that's the broken mast.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.680
Yeah, look, it's
got a hollow shadow in the middle.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:46.200
There’s a whole notch out in this.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.040
This is really where we see it in.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:50.240
Oh, oh, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.
Look at that.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:51.120
Look at it up here in the...

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.560
Wow look at the bow,
you can see the stern.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.320
Look it’s almost like a bow and a stern.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:56.720
Sometimes X marks the spot.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.120
Could that be the Beeswax?

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:00.080
It could be.

